{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Kleene Star.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Kleene Star.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"kleene star kleene star","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Kleene Star.md","fileName":"Kleene Star.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Kleene Star","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 22:54\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Regular Languages and Finite Automata]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Regular Languages and Finite Automata","Empty String","String","Alphabet","Regular Expression","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Kleene Star.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Kleene Star.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"kleene star kleene star","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Kleene Star.md","fileName":"Kleene Star.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Kleene Star","heading":"Kleene Star","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The Kleene star of a language S is S* = S⁰ ∪ S¹ ∪ S² ∪ …, the set of strings formed by concatenating zero or more strings from S. Because S⁰ = {ε}, every Kleene closure contains the [[Empty String]].\n\nWhen Σ is treated as a set of one-symbol strings, Σ* is exactly the set of all finite [[String|strings]] over the [[Alphabet]] Σ. The same operator supplies repetition in a [[Regular Expression]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Regular Languages and Finite Automata","Empty String","String","Alphabet","Regular Expression","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Kleene Star.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Kleene Star.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"kleene star kleene star","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Kleene Star.md","fileName":"Kleene Star.md","folder":"6 - Full No